{:edb/db-before-t 1562 :edb/db-after-t 1563 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534875 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:48:43.829Z" 13194139534875 true] [17592186147310 1100 "Let $y = \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y'-3y=0$.\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_n$, where $n=0,1,2, \\ldots$?" 13194139534875 true] [17592186147310 1100 "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y'-3y=0$.\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_n$, where $n=0,1,2 \\ldots$?" 13194139534875 false] [17592186147310 1145 "First, we find $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} - 3∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(m = 0)^(∞)(m + 1)a_{m + 1}x^{m} - 3∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} - 3∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0$, we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)3a_{n}x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} - 3a_{n}x^{n}] &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1} - 3a_{n}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+1) a_{n+1} -3 a_n = 0\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =\\dfrac{3 a_n}{n+1}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$" 13194139534875 false] [17592186147310 1145 "First, we find $y'(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n a_n x^{n-1}-3\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+1) a_{m+1} x^{m} -3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n =0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+1) a_{n+1} x^{n} -3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n =0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0$, we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-3{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}-3{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+1) a_{n+1}  -3 a_n = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =\\dfrac{3 a_n }{n+1}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2, \\ldots\n$$" 13194139534875 true] [17592186147311 1100 "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $2y''+y=0$. Find $f(n)$, given that the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$a_{n+2} = \\dfrac{a_n}{f(n)}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2\\ldots$" 13194139534875 false] [17592186147311 1100 "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $2y''+y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = \\dfrac{ a_n }{ f(n) }, \\qquad n= 0,1,2\\ldots\n$$" 13194139534875 true] [17592186147311 1145 "First, we find $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find $y''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″}(x) &= (∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{1} + ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1})^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 + ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)n(n - 1)a_{n}x^{n - 2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)n(n - 1)a_{n}x^{n - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y''(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty n (n-1) a_{n} x^{n-2} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-2$. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+2) (m+1) a_{m+2} x^{m} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+2) (n+1) a_{n+2} x^{n} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0$, we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)2(n + 2)(n + 1)a_{n + 2}x^{n} + ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[2(n + 2)(n + 1)a_{n + 2}x^{n} + a_{n}x^{n}] &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[2(n + 2)(n + 1)a_{n + 2} + a_{n}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n2(n+2)(n+1) a_{n+2} +a_n = 0\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = -\\dfrac{a_n}{2(n+1)(n+2)}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(n)=-2(n+1)(n+2)\n$$" 13194139534875 false] [17592186147311 1145 "First, we find $y'(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find $y''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y''(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty n (n-1) a_{n} x^{n-2} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+2) (m+1) a_{m+2} x^{m} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+2) (n+1) a_{n+2} x^{n} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}+{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n2(n+2)(n+1) a_{n+2}  +a_n = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = -\\dfrac{ a_n }{ 2(n+1)(n+2)  }, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(n)=-2(n+1)(n+2).\n$$" 13194139534875 true] [17592186147312 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/2ad87a5ee9c27f6d3f